---
# `moderate` plus two rules from stricter profiles, decided rather than deferred.
#
# `production` was rejected on purpose. It front-loads maximum lint friction onto
# the single highest-risk PR in this area, and its `ignore-errors` rule collides
# with preflight's deliberate collect-all-failures-then-aggregate pattern — which
# pushes an implementer toward either weakening the aggregate reporting or
# sprinkling `changed_when: false` where it is untrue. That is exactly how
# idempotence becomes a lie.
#
# The two rules enabled below are the ones that actually matter here: honest
# change reporting, and sane file modes on a tree that writes to /etc and /var.
profile: moderate

enable_list:
  - no-changed-when
  - risky-file-permissions

skip_list:
  # This tree uses ONE variable namespace, `lium_`, shared across every role on
  # purpose. The rule wants role-scoped prefixes (`kernel_grubcfg_path`,
  # `common_state_dir`), which exists to stop two roles colliding — but here the
  # sharing IS the design: `lium_state_dir`, `lium_proc_cmdline_path` and the
  # rest of the seams are read by common, kernel and verify alike, and renaming
  # them per role would mean three names for one thing.
  #
  # Note what is NOT skipped: `var-naming` itself stays on, so the `lium_`
  # prefix and casing are still enforced everywhere. Genuine role parameters do
  # carry their role's prefix — see `common_guard_reason` and
  # `common_guard_override_var` in site.yml.
  - var-naming[no-role-prefix]

exclude_paths:
  - tests/fixtures/
